inflation is surging around the world at  the fastest pace in decades but prices  are rising much faster in some countries  than in others even though all countries  are dealing with the same issues  disrupted supply chains higher fuel  costs and the end of covet 19 lockdowns  so what makes the difference let's look  at a few countries and some factors that  have contributed to their varying levels  of inflation  china the world's second largest economy  is seeing the lowest level of inflation  relative to its inflation rate before  the pandemic of any country in the world  in fact inflation was lower in china in  the second quarter of 2022 than it was  in the first quarter of 2020 but a major  reason for that is china's economy has  taken a big hit largely from coveted  lockdowns that began in early 2020 and  have continued through this year  the country's economy which had grown by  five to ten percent a year over the past  decade grew by just 0.4 percent in the  second quarter  and it's on pace for one of its weakest  growth years in recent history the  people's bank of china kept interest  rates higher than most throughout the  pandemic even as other central banks  were cutting rates to help boost their  economies  mexico is doing better than most  countries on inflation its inflation  rate in q2 was double what it was in the  first quarter of 2020 but that's well  below the average for large countries  around the world  its central bank started raising  interest rates in june 2021  well before many other central banks and  raised them faster mexico's government  spent relatively little in stimulus  refusing to borrow significant amounts  of money to underpin the economy even so  the mexican economy grew at a faster  pace in the second quarter than it did  in q1 2020 and grew at almost four times  the rate of other large countries in the  organization of economic cooperation and  development or oecd  inflation in the united states has more  than quadrupled from 